<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gregory of Tours</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 538–594</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gregory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> was a Gallo-Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Tours from 573 and the author of the Histories, often called the History of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>. His work is the main source for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a> and the Merovingian kings, and for many small kingdoms and rulers of 5th- and 6th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> that are otherwise almost unknown. He also records events such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hygelac">Hygelac</a>'s raid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours.jpg" alt="Gregory of Tours" width="320" height="711" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gregory_of_Tours_cour_Napoleon_Louvre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
